#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171688 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "416faff8-3422-5bb0-9062-571ccfb18093" :question/math-academy-id "e-10094" :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.25$, and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is given by $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$.*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7 x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 0.7$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.25}{42}} \\approx 0.3$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 0.7 \\pm 0.3$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171696 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "4d33f1a3-c51f-5a97-a498-def7a8364d81" :question/math-academy-id "e-11098" :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they finally obtained on that exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5$, and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by*\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$." :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x = 21.3 + 10x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 10$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.05} = 2.92$.Computing $S_{xx}$, we get\n$∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}|= (4 - 5)^{2} + (4.5 - 5)^{2} + (5.5 - 5)^{2} + (6 - 5)^{2}; = 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 1; = 2.5$. Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 2.92 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.375}{2.5}} \\approx 2.2$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 10 \\pm 2.2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c93c89a1-098f-50a0-ac3a-08dc83a84320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168886} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8140f2-ef6b-5dd8-a2ff-decbe7e83052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168887} {:answer/id #uuid "414e1bd2-3ced-5d49-bc43-dff07a90e6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168888} {:answer/id #uuid "4d80f3c6-82c6-521a-a2a2-b01992d77cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168889} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd0969e-f100-53ab-a342-238468fad53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168890}] :answer-field/id #uuid "10137e41-2459-5b06-b3a4-3230a1046efa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168885}] :question/id #uuid "1ac4a55b-9276-584c-aabe-f3000169b367" :question/math-academy-id "q-143260" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $115$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of four children. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $y^(^) = 80.6 + 5x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 6$, and $MSE = 0.35$, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 4.303) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by*\n$SE[β^(^)_{1}] = \\sqrt{\\frac{MSE}{S_{xx}}}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be3ed5e4-db77-5844-99eb-2373d53d4fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169096} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a9c14b-c29f-53d1-8434-7046339a3295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169097} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea68b01-435a-5d11-aa1b-81b29b61b328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169098} {:answer/id #uuid "54528e56-5dcd-5b9e-93f9-76d58c3b369a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169099} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6e5462-6938-5d3e-9d9a-873923c367fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169100}] :answer-field/id #uuid "618d8cc5-09ee-52cf-bb55-128c57e46ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169095}] :question/id #uuid "2d548f76-b08c-5cf0-ae75-59db6390b2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159571" :question/problem "| **Production ($x$)** | **$24$** | **$27$** | **$30$** | **$31$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Price per $kg$ ($y$)** | $2$ | $1.6$ | $1.4$ | $1.4$ |\n\nThe table above shows wheat production, in tons, and the average price of a kilogram of flour, in dollars, for a given country over the last four years. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $y^(^) = 4 - 0.1x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 28$, and $MSE = 0.007$, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by*\n$SE[β^(^)_{1}] = \\sqrt{\\frac{MSE}{S_{xx}}}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
